From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: blurbery <271863866+blurbery@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] render: keep unresolved glyphs per event and refresh them
 after new fallback faces

Applies to libass 0.17.5 after patches 0001 and 0002, and changes only
the event render cache that 0002 adds and the counters it needs.

- Output with glyphs that no font face could provide is never shared
  between events. A glyph counts as unresolved when ass_font_get_index()
  ends without a glyph for a printable character, after its fallback
  search. The shared cache entry for such output only records that, and
  each event keeps its own entry, under an owner id held in its
  ASS_RenderPriv and the number of fallback faces added to any font when
  its render started. Another event with the same text therefore renders
  its own text once, with the faces that exist then, instead of
  inheriting missing glyphs.
- Once any font gains a fallback face, each such event is rendered once
  more on its next frame (on the same frame if it is rendered after the
  face was added), so glyphs the new face covers appear when unpatched
  libass shows them. That costs at most one extra render, and one change
  report, per added face, and nothing while no faces are added. An event
  whose glyphs cannot be resolved is not searched for again on later
  frames. Entries without unresolved glyphs are shared and kept as
  before: a new face is appended, so glyphs that were found keep their
  face.
- The cache key includes the event's Effect field. Without it an event
  with a Banner or Scroll effect could reuse the static output of an
  earlier event with the same text, and stand still.
- The cache is emptied whenever the state generation changes, because
  entries of an older generation can never match again. 0002 already
  empties it when the renderer is reconfigured; this also releases it
  after changes that only change the generation, such as the shaper, line
  spacing, margin use or the track's styles. The 32 MiB budget of 0002
  still bounds it in between.
- Image clones copy the type of the image they clone, which 0002 leaves
  unset.

Everything else behaves as with 0002: static output that has not changed
is reported unchanged, and every real change is reported with the same
value as before.

